This chapter covers a pattern that I use quite often when working on personal projects as it scales decently well and keeps the code relatively clean.

One thing the article doesn't go into is *why* it creates two separate contexts for the `TasksContext` and `TasksDispatchContext` so I figured I'd note that here. The problem with context is that when a component uses the `useContext` hook to read the state, any changes to that state will cause a rerender even if the component is only interested in a particular slice of that state. So by creating two contexts we get to rerender only components that are interested in the `tasks` state and components that are only responsible for dispatching actions can avoid an unnecessary rerender for any state changes to the `tasks` state. This is a scalability problem you need to consider with context as overuse of the context api can lead to unnecessary rerenders and if your app has problems with this you might consider using a library that allows for reading only particular slices of state without unnecessary rerenders like Zustand. Most apps don't need a special state management library which is why I typically just use context for my personal projects.